Remembering the Holocaust: A Lesson to All of Humankind\hspace{0pt}

\begin{quote}
To the new generation rising from the twenty-first century, the
Holocaust may feel like\hspace{0pt}nothing more than just a distant,
painful memory. Many of us are lucky enough to have
never\hspace{0pt}experienced anything as horrifying as the many
atrocities committed during this time. Still, it's\hspace{0pt}important
to remember that the society and background from which the Holocaust
emerged\hspace{0pt}wasn't as distant from ourselves as we think. The
Holocaust was not only a campaign led by\hspace{0pt}Nazi authorities--
it was also a result of the actions and inactions of everyday people as
they\hspace{0pt}witnessed the inhumane actions of those in
charge.\hspace{0pt}\\
Many targeted individuals spoke out against the injustice. Altogether,
1.5 million Jews\hspace{0pt}retaliated by fighting with the Allies or in
secret partisan organizations. After learning that
the\hspace{0pt}so-called ``camps'' were actually graveyards, many Jews
revolted as a last act of resistance. Out\hspace{0pt}of the many
uprisings that happened, the most prominent example would be the Warsaw
Ghetto\hspace{0pt}Uprising of 1943. A 23-year-old Jew named Mordecai
Anielewicz formed the ZOB, or Jewish\hspace{0pt}Fighting Organization,
after learning of the killing center they would be sent to.
Throughout\hspace{0pt}1943, the ZOB resisted German authorities by
firing on German troops during a round-up of\hspace{0pt}ghetto residents
to a killing center. The Warsaw Ghetto Uprising lasted for one month,
with\hspace{0pt}
\end{quote}

1\hspace{0pt}

\begin{quote}
7,000 Jews dying while fighting or hiding. Targeted individuals started
out as ordinary people\hspace{0pt}who ended up fighting to their last
breath with the honor of a warrior.\hspace{0pt}\\
In addition to the directly targeted Jews, a few individuals resisted as
well. Made up of\hspace{0pt}German citizens, the White Rose Movement
distributed anti-Nazi papers to combat Nazi\hspace{0pt}propaganda.
Protestant residents in the southern French village of Le
Chambon-sur-Lignon hid\hspace{0pt}thousands of Jews, many of them
children, in homes, convents, and monasteries.
French\hspace{0pt}clergymen sheltered 12,000 Jewish children then
smuggled them into neutral Switzerland and\hspace{0pt}Spain. Even Warsaw
Zoo director Jan Zabinski saved many Polish Jews by hiding them in
the\hspace{0pt}zoo. These individuals were motivated by personal morals
or religious conviction. Still, they\hspace{0pt}were taking an
incredible risk-- many helpers were put into concentration camps as
prisoners,\hspace{0pt}arrested, or immediately executed due to treason.
Meanwhile, the majority of people who chose\hspace{0pt}to stay silent
and do nothing were silenced by fear. Staying silent never helps the
victim; it only\hspace{0pt}makes it easier for the
perpetrator.\hspace{0pt}\\
The choices of nations on whether to act or not to act also deeply
impacted the outcome\hspace{0pt}of the Holocaust. From early on, British
and American intelligence knew of genocide in\hspace{0pt}German-occupied
eastern Europe through intercepted German radio. Despite this, the
efforts\hspace{0pt}made by the Allies to further stop the Holocaust were
disappointing. By 1938, a quarter of the\hspace{0pt}Jewish population in
Germany had fled the country. Such a large influx of Jewish
immigrants\hspace{0pt}were unwelcome in many countries, including the
United States. In a 1938 survey of Americans,\hspace{0pt}69\%
disapproved of accepting German Jewish refugees. This shows that
antisemitism was not\hspace{0pt}limited to Nazi Germany.\hspace{0pt}\\
In the summer of 1938, representatives from 32 nations met at the
nine-day Evian\hspace{0pt}Conference to discuss Jewish immigration. Out
of them, the Dominican Republic was the only\hspace{0pt}
\end{quote}

2\hspace{0pt}

Jia\hspace{0pt}\hspace{0pt}3\hspace{0pt}

\begin{quote}
China tire tariff saved 1,200 jobs in the domestic tire production
industry but lost 3,731 retail\hspace{0pt}jobs. In 2018, Trump's China
tariff added a few steel and aluminum jobs, but lost 75,000 jobs
in\hspace{0pt}consumer firms of steel and aluminum (Hebert). In all, the
2018 US-China trade war cost the\hspace{0pt}American economy 300,000
jobs, nearly 0.3\% of real GDP, and firms lost at least \$1.7 trillion
in\hspace{0pt}stocks (``Trump's Trade War Has Killed 300,000 Jobs'').
Factories didn't even move back to the\hspace{0pt}United States-- they
simply moved from China to other countries in Southeast Asia. In the
end,\hspace{0pt}American consumers paid the higher cost for no
return.\hspace{0pt}\\
Many examples have demonstrated that tariffs
don't\hspace{0pt}\hspace{0pt}\\
do a very good job of improving the\hspace{0pt}American economy---at
least, not by themselves. Historically, tariffs like the Reconstruction
Era\hspace{0pt}tariffs did indeed contribute to growth in the American
economy by developing the American\hspace{0pt}steel industry to match
those of European powers. But, although tariffs can buy time
for\hspace{0pt}domestic industries to gain a footing on international
competition, they are not sustainable.\hspace{0pt}Today, the United
States still relies heavily on tariffs. Does this mean that we are
falling behind?\hspace{0pt} Some short-term alternatives to tariffs
include minimizing the \$297,000 to \$400,000 that\hspace{0pt}American
firms each spend annually to meet regulations set by the government
(Hebert) or\hspace{0pt}lowering taxes on businesses so they won't raise
their product prices so high. That being said,\hspace{0pt}both of these
alternatives have side effects in the long run.\hspace{0pt}\\
To this, there is one more, long-term solution: investing in American
education to\hspace{0pt}develop the necessary manufacturing skills in
our workforce. 21st century America needs to\hspace{0pt}protect its
manufacturing capabilities. Many studies have proven that American
public education\hspace{0pt}has fallen behind other nations.
Furthermore, many students are enrolled in four-year
universities\hspace{0pt}when it isn't necessarily the best suit for
them, nor for the workforce as a whole. Only 64.7\hspace{0pt}percent of
students who started college in 2016 have graduated by now, which is
already the\hspace{0pt}
\end{quote}

Jia\hspace{0pt}\hspace{0pt}4\hspace{0pt}

\begin{quote}
greatest percentage cohort as of 2025 (``Yearly Progress and
Completion''). By offering\hspace{0pt}\\
incentives to encourage job training instead of pushing every student
toward a bachelor's degree,\hspace{0pt}the US could diversify its
workforce and bring new skill sets to the table. While tariffs may
buy\hspace{0pt}time for domestic industries, long-term success depends
on the hard work and productivity of the\hspace{0pt}American
people.\hspace{0pt}\\
Ultimately, while tariffs may provide short-term relief, they are not
sustainable in the\hspace{0pt}long run. Americans face higher prices and
limited job growth when tariffs are applied by\hspace{0pt}themselves.
However, if tariffs are combined with improving public education and
the\hspace{0pt}\\
workforce, Americans won't have to choose between patriotism and their
economic goals when\hspace{0pt}buying a car---they will simply choose
American.\hspace{0pt}
\end{quote}

Jia\hspace{0pt}\hspace{0pt}5\hspace{0pt}

